---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";

const install = `pip install 'power-pptx[embedding]'
python -m playwright install chromium`;
const deck = `from pathlib import Path
from power_pptx.compose import from_html

# One visible, 1280 × 720 <section data-slide> per slide.
prs = from_html(
    Path("deck.html"),
    selector="[data-slide]",
    viewport=(1280, 720), slide_size="16:9",
    device_scale_factor=2, fit="contain",
    # For asynchronous charts: wait_for_function="window.deckReady === true"
)
prs.save("deck.pptx")`;
const element = `from power_pptx import BBox, add_html_figure, add_svg_figure

picture = add_html_figure(
    slide, "diagram.html", *BBox.from_inches(1, 1.5, 8, 4.5),
    selector="#architecture", fit="contain", omit_background=True,
)
picture.alt_text = "Request flow through the gateway to services."

# Native SVG in modern Office, with a PNG companion for other renderers.
add_svg_figure(slide, "logo.svg", *BBox.from_inches(0.5, 0.5, 2, 1))`;
---

<DocsLayout title="HTML & SVG Embedding"
  description="Use styled HTML as a slide design reference, capture sections, and combine native content with embedded SVG or browser visuals.">
  <p class="lead">
    A good HTML design gives you the typography, spacing, palette, and composition for a deck.
    Preserve those choices when building native slides, browser captures, or a hybrid.
  </p>
  <h2 id="choose">Choose the right output</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Workflow</th><th>Editability</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Content that changes</td><td>Rebuild HTML layout with native APIs</td><td>Editable text, cards, tables and charts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Browser appearance</td><td><code>compose.from_html</code></td><td>One static picture per section; text also in notes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Complex artwork with editable copy</td><td>Native text plus SVG/HTML figures</td><td>Native content with embedded assets</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    HTML capture preserves browser pixels. It does not translate arbitrary CSS into editable
    PowerPoint shapes. The bundled skill's <code>content-embedding.md</code> guide covers the
    native reconstruction workflow and <code>examples/html_embedding/</code> builds both versions.
  </p>
  <h2 id="install">Optional dependencies</h2>
  <Code code={install} />
  <p>
    The <code>[html]</code> and <code>[svg]</code> extras are also available separately.
    An existing Chromium can be selected with <code>browser_path</code> or
    <code>POWER_PPTX_CHROMIUM</code>. These APIs are new in this source tree; check availability
    when using a pinned older package.
  </p>
  <h2 id="sections">Styled sections into a deck</h2>
  <Code code={deck} />
  <p>
    Sections are captured in DOM order with one browser session. Keep them visible in normal
    document flow, using fixed dimensions matching the deck. Local CSS, images, and fonts under
    the HTML directory are served temporarily on localhost. Inline markup should use data URLs
    or absolute asset URLs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Capture waits for decoded images and ready web fonts, disables CSS animations, and hides
    carets. Use an explicit ready condition for application-rendered charts. Container overflow
    raises by default; <code>check_overflow=False</code> permits intentional overflow. The
    default wait is <code>load</code>; choose <code>networkidle</code> explicitly when needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Visible text is copied to notes and picture alt text. <code>data-title</code>,
    <code>aria-label</code>, or the first heading names each picture. Notes preserve content
    but do not make text inside the picture selectable or editable.
  </p>
  <h2 id="assets">Embed individual visuals</h2>
  <Code code={element} />
  <p>
    <code>contain</code> preserves proportions, <code>cover</code> crops to fill, and
    <code>stretch</code> changes proportions. Capture density changes resolution, not physical
    size. A CSS selector must match one visible element. JSON figure specs and
    <code>figure_slide</code> forward capture settings through <code>html_options</code>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="svg">Portable SVG</h2>
  <p>
    SVG inputs accept markup, bytes, paths, and binary streams. Repeated identical media are
    shared across slides. Use self-contained SVG with a <code>viewBox</code> and embedded image
    resources. Web fonts, scripts, <code>foreignObject</code>, and complex CSS filters can differ
    between Office and CairoSVG; capture the containing HTML for those visuals. An explicit
    <code>png_fallback</code> can provide a high-resolution raster companion.
  </p>
  <h2 id="verify">Compare the rendered result</h2>
  <p>
    Keep editable text and business data native where useful. Map browser bounds relative to each
    section to <code>BBox</code>, using the deck's real dimensions. At 1280px → 13⅓ inches, 64px
    CSS type maps to 48pt. Run native layout checks, then render and compare with the HTML reference:
    the linter cannot inspect text inside a picture. A long <code>full_page</code> screenshot is
    one image; it does not paginate a webpage.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
